WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.487
Noise-induced hearing loss is one of the most common

00:07.487 --> 00:09.925
health problems in mining, and it's completely

00:09.925 --> 00:12.925
preventable. It doesn't hurt. It happens a little

00:12.925 --> 00:16.550
at a time, shift after shift, as loud noise damages

00:16.550 --> 00:19.275
tiny hair cells in your inner ear. Those cells

00:19.275 --> 00:21.788
don't grow back. By the time you notice it,

00:21.788 --> 00:23.387
the damage is permanent.

00:24.516 --> 00:27.441
Watch for the early signs. Ringing or buzzing in

00:27.441 --> 00:30.654
your ears after a shift. Voices that sound muffled.

00:30.654 --> 00:33.578
Turning the TV up louder than your family likes.

00:33.578 --> 00:36.616
Trouble following a conversation in a noisy room.

00:36.616 --> 00:39.166
Ringing that goes away overnight is a warning that

00:39.166 --> 00:42.141
your ears were overloaded. If it keeps happening,

00:42.141 --> 00:43.741
the damage is adding up.

00:44.874 --> 00:47.237
Here's a simple test. If you have to raise your

00:47.237 --> 00:49.612
voice to be understood by someone an arm's length

00:49.612 --> 00:52.825
away, the noise is loud enough to damage your hearing

00:52.825 --> 00:55.062
over time. Protect your ears.

00:58.384 --> 01:00.896
At every one of our sites, the loudest places are

01:00.896 --> 01:03.659
the plant and screens, and underground,

01:03.659 --> 01:06.859
the mine fans. Other work gets loud too:

01:06.859 --> 01:09.821
the drill, grinders and air tools in the shop,

01:09.821 --> 01:12.334
and mobile equipment with the doors open or a bad

01:12.334 --> 01:15.409
muffler. At all of our sites, hearing protection

01:15.409 --> 01:17.921
is required around the plant while it's running

01:17.921 --> 01:20.396
and around the mine fans. Your instructor will

01:20.396 --> 01:22.346
show you where those areas start.

01:23.470 --> 01:26.595
The rule puts controls first. Engineering controls

01:26.595 --> 01:29.833
reduce the noise itself: enclosures around loud

01:29.833 --> 01:32.983
equipment, sound-dampened cabs, mufflers,

01:32.983 --> 01:35.645
rubber liners in chutes, and good maintenance,

01:35.645 --> 01:38.670
because worn bearings and loose guards get louder.

01:38.670 --> 01:41.608
Administrative controls limit how long people spend

01:41.608 --> 01:44.883
in loud places. When an operator uses administrative

01:44.883 --> 01:47.520
controls, the procedures are posted on the mine

01:47.520 --> 01:50.208
bulletin board. Hearing protectors are your last

01:50.208 --> 01:53.545
line of defense, not the first. So your part includes

01:53.545 --> 01:55.858
keeping cab doors and windows shut,

01:55.858 --> 01:58.433
and reporting a broken muffler, a missing panel

01:58.433 --> 02:01.345
on an enclosure or a machine that's suddenly louder

02:01.345 --> 02:02.546
than it used to be.

02:03.676 --> 02:06.063
Your ears don't know whether noise comes from work

02:06.063 --> 02:08.863
or from home. Chainsaws, lawn mowers,

02:08.863 --> 02:12.151
motorcycles, shooting and loud music in earbuds

02:12.151 --> 02:15.038
all add up on the same hair cells. Protect your

02:15.038 --> 02:17.738
hearing at home the same way you do on site.

02:17.738 --> 02:20.401
And before a hearing test, staying away from loud

02:20.401 --> 02:23.339
noise at home matters just as much as at work.

02:26.654 --> 02:30.516
MSHA's noise standard is 30 CFR Part 62.

02:30.516 --> 02:32.779
It's based on your average noise exposure over

02:32.779 --> 02:36.666
an 8-hour shift. At an average of 85 decibels,

02:36.666 --> 02:39.416
the action level, the operator has to enroll you

02:39.416 --> 02:42.454
in a hearing conservation program. At 90,

02:42.454 --> 02:45.041
the permissible exposure level, the operator has

02:45.041 --> 02:48.066
to use all feasible engineering and administrative

02:48.066 --> 02:50.529
controls to bring your exposure down,

02:50.529 --> 02:53.491
and keep you in the program. Above 105,

02:53.491 --> 02:55.591
you wear an earplug and an earmuff at the same

02:55.591 --> 02:59.191
time. And no miner is ever exposed to sound above

02:59.191 --> 03:02.754
115 decibels, measured without counting hearing

03:02.754 --> 03:03.716
protection.

03:04.832 --> 03:06.757
If you're enrolled in the hearing conservation

03:06.757 --> 03:09.519
program, you get a baseline hearing test within

03:09.519 --> 03:12.457
six months of enrollment, or within twelve months

03:12.457 --> 03:15.607
if a mobile test van is used. Before the baseline

03:15.607 --> 03:19.032
test, avoid loud noise for at least 14 hours,

03:19.032 --> 03:21.494
or wear your hearing protection, so the test shows

03:21.494 --> 03:23.969
your real hearing. After that, you're offered a

03:23.969 --> 03:26.582
hearing test at least every 12 months,

03:26.582 --> 03:29.257
at no cost to you. Each test is compared to your

03:29.257 --> 03:31.932
baseline, so a change can be caught early.

03:31.932 --> 03:34.582
You also get training on noise and hearing protectors

03:34.582 --> 03:37.682
within 30 days of enrollment and every year after

03:37.682 --> 03:39.082
that while you're in the program.

03:40.202 --> 03:42.514
If your exposure reaches the action level,

03:42.514 --> 03:44.914
the operator provides hearing protectors at no

03:44.914 --> 03:47.814
cost, and you get to choose from at least two kinds

03:47.814 --> 03:50.577
of earmuffs and two kinds of earplugs.

03:50.577 --> 03:53.652
Pick the one you'll actually wear. For foam plugs,

03:53.652 --> 03:55.914
roll the plug into a thin cylinder,

03:55.914 --> 03:58.327
reach over your head and pull the top of your ear

03:58.327 --> 04:01.439
up and back, slide the plug in, and hold it while

04:01.439 --> 04:04.764
it expands. A plug that's only halfway in protects

04:04.764 --> 04:07.627
only halfway. Muffs need a clean seal,

04:07.627 --> 04:10.539
so safety glasses with thin temples and no hat

04:10.539 --> 04:12.289
under the cushions work best.

04:13.424 --> 04:16.200
Plugs and muffs together are only required above

04:16.200 --> 04:19.250
that 105 level, and none of our sites uses double

04:19.250 --> 04:21.975
protection today. What matters most is wearing

04:21.975 --> 04:24.774
your protection the whole time you're in the noise.

04:24.774 --> 04:27.112
Taking it off for even a few minutes in a very

04:27.112 --> 04:29.787
loud area lets in a large share of the shift's

04:29.787 --> 04:33.300
dose. Keep plugs clean, replace worn-out cushions

04:33.300 --> 04:36.200
and damaged plugs, and ask for new ones.

04:36.200 --> 04:37.062
They're free.

04:38.187 --> 04:40.349
Some people skip hearing protection because they're

04:40.349 --> 04:43.599
afraid they won't hear a backup alarm or a shout.

04:43.599 --> 04:46.937
In very loud places, protectors often make speech

04:46.937 --> 04:49.549
and alarms easier to pick out, because they cut

04:49.549 --> 04:52.099
the background roar. Use your radio,

04:52.099 --> 04:54.374
make eye contact with equipment operators,

04:54.374 --> 04:56.937
and keep your eyes up. If you truly can't hear

04:56.937 --> 04:58.974
something you need to hear while protected,

04:58.974 --> 05:01.999
tell your supervisor. The fix is a different protector

05:01.999 --> 05:04.674
or a different procedure, not bare ears.

05:05.805 --> 05:08.105
Hearing protection on around the plant while it's

05:08.105 --> 05:11.292
running and around the mine fans. Fit it right

05:11.292 --> 05:13.579
and wear it the whole time you're in the noise.

05:13.579 --> 05:16.430
Report a bad muffler, a missing enclosure or a

05:16.430 --> 05:19.092
cab door that won't close, because controls come

05:19.092 --> 05:22.430
first. And get your hearing tested every year.

05:22.430 --> 05:24.767
Hearing you lose at work doesn't come back.

05:25.898 --> 05:29.060
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:29.060 --> 05:30.560
override this video.
